          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does exercise help knee osteoarthritis or make it worse?",
          "answer": "Exercise is beneficial for managing knee osteoarthritis. According to the guidelines, structured exercise, which includes muscle strengthening, range-of-motion work, and aerobic activity, is the core treatment for this condition. This approach has strong support from evidence showing that it can produce effects comparable to medication, while also having a better safety profile. It's important to note that exercise is recommended regardless of the severity of the condition or the level of pain experienced at the beginning. Pain during exercise, as long as it is within tolerable limits, is not necessarily a sign of further harm (source: \"Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options\").\n\nThe focus on exercise is to reduce pain, improve function, and maintain independence. Therefore, rather than worsening the condition, exercise is a key part of effective management (source: \"Osteoarthritis\").\n\nI could not cover specific exercise programs or techniques, or any personal experiences related to exercise and knee osteoarthritis. Please consult with a healthcare professional for personalized recommendations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
